A||||9||William Federle||Headlight problems||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||06-22-2000||11:08 PM||||I just rebuilt my right hand headlight motor. I put a new plastic gear and rubber-bumpers and a new brush/high torque switch assembly in it. I put it back together and reinstalled it in my car. I cranked it up manually untill I heard it click. Then I carefully connected the blue wire and turned it on. (I have a switch to prevent the lights from going down and that switch was in the position that used to keep them up). When I turned on the headlights, the right one went down right away. I wasn't able to get it to go up. <P>My question is, are the brush/high torque switch assemblies for a particular side only - that is, is there a certain one for the right and a certain one for the left?<P>Thanks in advance for wading through this and giving me a reply.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||ROB MAC||06-23-2000||01:47 PM||||If I remember correctly they are the same. Your problem may be with the relay. Try switching them and see if the left side stays down. Once I rebuilt a motor and the same thing happened: went down and wouldn't go up. The "C" clip snapped inside.||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||06-23-2000||01:58 PM||||Thanks. I'll try the relay swap.||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||06-24-2000||03:49 PM||||I swapped actuator relays. The problem stays on the right side. I then rebuilt the left motor and as I took it apart, compared it with the right hand motor. I think I put the right hand motor back together right. When I got the left motor back in, it only went up. When I swapped the relays, it still only went up. Does this mean that the isolation relay is bad?<P>I don't think I changed the orientation of the magnet in either motor.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ROB MAC||06-25-2000||12:38 AM||||You say you rebuilt the right and it didn't work properly and then you rebuilt the left and it now does not work right ,I would tend to think something is not right in the motor. Was the left working before you rebuilt it?||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||06-25-2000||08:12 AM||||No, neither was working. I got the car last August and since then till last month, they both worked but sometimes I had to turn the knob on the left one a little bit to get it started up. Then last month I had to do this on both to get them started down. Then they didn't work at all. <P>I took apart the right motor and found the plastic gear missing a tooth and the rubber bumpers were in pieces. <P>I rebuilt the right hand one and tried it out without the left hand one hooked up. It only went down, not up. Then, thinking maybe I needed both motors hooked up, I rebuilt the left motor. It also had a tooth missing from the plastic gear and the rubber bumpers were disintegrated - it was like green soup in the bottom of the motor.<P> I got it back together and now it will go up, but not down.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-25-2000||09:35 AM||||Either a relay or one of the contacts might be dirty.  (I've given this some thought.)  I'm not sure if a relay would do anything like that, but if both of them are doing the same thing...Could you have a broken wire somewhere?  You could try checking the wires when you operated the headlight switch.  Just trying to give you some suggestions.  Neither of the motors in the Monkeymobile worked until I rebuilt them and then both worked fine (mostly) and the motors in the Grape Ape have been good so far (knock on wood!).||||reg||1
Z||000007||William Federle||06-25-2000||11:39 AM||||Thanks MM. That's one of the things I was wondering about. If you needed both motors in for operation. <P>I forgot to mention in provious posts that my right headlight goes down when I turn the lights on. I think the polarity is getting reversed for that motor when it shouldn't be. <P>I ordered the isolation relay and two actuator relays from the Fiero store yesterday so hopefully by the end of this next week I'll know if that's the answer. I don't think the actuator relays are bad yet, but I just wanted to start out with a clean slate with all three so that the relays will be out of the picture if this fix doesn't work. Believe it or not the isolator relay is $5.00 cheaper than a local GM dealer's parts dept on the isolation relay.<P>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 06-25-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 06-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Taijiguy||06-27-2000||08:16 AM||||Did you remove the over-ride swithc you installed? If you installed it to keep the lights up while they were inoperative, you might have mis-wired something there. It may have worked fine for keeping them up, but now it could be creating problems with functional motors. It does sound like you got some wires crossed someplace. <BR>Just another suggestion....||||reg||1
Z||000009||William Federle||06-29-2000||11:18 PM||||Well, I got it fixed - sort of. The right hand motor was going the wrong direction when I turned the lites on. This turned out to be because I got the magnet turned over while I had the motor apart. The rotation was correct after I opened the motor again and turned the magnet over.<P>The other piece or two of the puzzle was a defective isolation relay - replacing it made the left-hand motor go down as well as up, and the right hand motor needed one last thing - a new actuator relay. <P>The only thing is that after I turn the lites off and they go down, I hear the relays and motor switches cycling . It also happens when the lights are on, but the engine is usually running then, and the consequences aren't as drastic as they are for the situation where the lights are off.<BR>I think that the motor is being pushed back by the wind-up of the linkage after the hi-torque switch turns it off. Then it moves slightly to re-make the switch and so on. I solved this (for the lites off situation) by shutting off my switch that prevents the lights from lowering after being shut off. That switch functions by interrupting the white wire that connects +12 Volts to the opposite side of the motor when the lites are shut off. Without the 12 volts on this wire with the lites down, the switches and relays don't continually cycle. <P>I think I need new motors as a final fix.<P>Thanks to all who made suggestions.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 06-29-2000).]||||reg||1
